Non-Recursive (Iterative) Inorder Traversal using Stack:
```c
void iterativeInorder(struct Node *root) {
    struct Node *stack[100];
    int top = -1;
    struct Node *curr = root;
    while (curr != NULL || top != -1) {
        while (curr != NULL) {
            stack[++top] = curr; // Push along left spine
            curr = curr->left;
        }
        curr = stack[top--]; // Pop
        printf("%d ", curr->data);
        curr = curr->right;
    }
}
```

BST Search & Insertion Algorithms:
- Search: Compare target with `root->data`. If equal, found. If `target < root->data`, recurse left; else recurse right. Time: $O(h)$ where $h$ is tree height ($O(\log n)$ for balanced, $O(n)$ for skewed).
- Insertion:
```c
struct Node* insertBST(struct Node* root, int key) {
    if (root == NULL) {
        struct Node* n = (struct Node*)malloc(sizeof(struct Node));
        n->data = key; n->left = n->right = NULL;
        return n;
    }
    if (key < root->data) root->left = insertBST(root->left, key);
    else if (key > root->data) root->right = insertBST(root->right, key);
    return root;
}
```

BST Deletion Algorithm (3 Critical Cases - KTU Repeated Question):
1. Case 1 (Leaf Node - 0 Children): Simply unlink the node from its parent and `free()` memory.
2. Case 2 (Single Child): Bypass the node by linking its parent directly to its only child, then `free()` the target node.
3. Case 3 (Two Children): The node cannot be directly removed without breaking tree connectivity. We must find either its Inorder Successor (smallest value in its right subtree: go right once, then left all the way) or Inorder Predecessor (largest value in left subtree). Copy the successor's data into the target node, then recursively delete the Inorder Successor from the right subtree (which is guaranteed to have at most one child).
```c
struct Node* deleteBST(struct Node* root, int key) {
    if (root == NULL) return root;
    if (key < root->data) root->left = deleteBST(root->left, key);
    else if (key > root->data) root->right = deleteBST(root->right, key);
    else {
        if (root->left == NULL) { struct Node* temp = root->right; free(root); return temp; }
        else if (root->right == NULL) { struct Node* temp = root->left; free(root); return temp; }
        struct Node* succ = root->right;
        while (succ->left != NULL) succ = succ->left; // Find Inorder Successor
        root->data = succ->data;
        root->right = deleteBST(root->right, succ->data);
    }
    return root;
}
```

Heapify-Down (Max-Heapify) Algorithm:
```c
void maxHeapify(int arr[], int n, int i) {
    int largest = i;
    int left = 2 * i + 1;
    int right = 2 * i + 2;
    if (left < n && arr[left] > arr[largest]) largest = left;
    if (right < n && arr[right] > arr[largest]) largest = right;
    if (largest != i) {
        int temp = arr[i]; arr[i] = arr[largest]; arr[largest] = temp;
        maxHeapify(arr, n, largest);
    }
}
```

Depth-First Search (DFS):
- Explores deeply along each branch before backtracking.
- Data Structure: Explicit/Call Stack + `visited[]` array.
- Recursive DFS Algorithm:
```c
void DFS(int u) {
    visited[u] = true;
    printf("%d ", u);
    for (struct Node* p = adj[u]; p != NULL; p = p->next) {
        int v = p->dest;
        if (!visited[v]) DFS(v);
    }
}
```
- Time Complexity: $O(V + E)$ with Adjacency List; $O(V^2)$ with Adjacency Matrix. Space: $O(V)$ (recursion stack recursion depth).
- Key Applications: Cycle detection, topological sorting in DAGs, finding strongly connected components (Kosaraju/Tarjan).

Partitioning Mechanisms (Lomuto vs Hoare):
- Lomuto Partition (Easier, chosen pivot = last element `A[r]`):
```c
int partition(int A[], int p, int r) {
    int pivot = A[r], i = p - 1;
    for (int j = p; j < r; j++) {
        if (A[j] <= pivot) {
            i++;
            int t = A[i]; A[i] = A[j]; A[j] = t;
        }
    }
    int t = A[i + 1]; A[i + 1] = A[r]; A[r] = t;
    return i + 1;
}
```
- Hoare Partition (Original, two pointers inwards from ends): Employs fewer swaps on average (~3x fewer than Lomuto).

2-Way Merge Algorithm (C Implementation):
```c
void merge(int arr[], int l, int m, int r) {
    int n1 = m - l + 1, n2 = r - m;
    int L[n1], R[n2];
    for (int i = 0; i < n1; i++) L[i] = arr[l + i];
    for (int j = 0; j < n2; j++) R[j] = arr[m + 1 + j];
    int i = 0, j = 0, k = l;
    while (i < n1 && j < n2) {
        if (L[i] <= R[j]) arr[k++] = L[i++];
        else arr[k++] = R[j++];
    }
    while (i < n1) arr[k++] = L[i++];
    while (j < n2) arr[k++] = R[j++];
}
```
- Complexity: Time is strictly $\Theta(n \log n)$ in Best, Worst, and Average cases ($T(n) = 2T(n/2) + O(n)$). Space is $\Theta(n)$ auxiliary memory (not in-place). It is Stable.
